<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arab–Byzantine wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">629–1050s</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arab–Byzantine wars were fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> and their successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> from 629 into the 11th century. In the first decades the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, most seriously in 717–718. The frontier then settled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, where raiding continued until the Byzantines recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> (961), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> (969) in the 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arab-byzantine-wars.jpg" alt="Arab–Byzantine wars" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greekfire-madridskylitzes1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The long Byzantine-Sasanian wars of the 6th and 7th centuries and recurring outbreaks of plague left both empires exhausted. The last of those wars ended with a Byzantine victory, when Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> regained all lost territory and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 629. Neither empire had time to recover before meeting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, newly united by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The first skirmishes came under Muhammad, and the Battle of Mu'tah followed the murder of his envoy by the Ghassanids, a Byzantine vassal kingdom. Muhammad died in 632, and his successor Abu Bakr ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a> in control of the whole peninsula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 634 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Heraclius was ill and could not lead his army in person. The Rashidun army won near Ajnadayn in the summer of 634 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>. Syria was captured in 639 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 642, and the Exarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> fell gradually between 647 and 670. Arab fleets entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> from the 650s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, who succeeded the Rashidun in 661, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 674 to 678 and again in 717 to 718, and failed both times.</p><p>After the second siege the frontier settled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, where raids and counter-raids became nearly ritualised and the border region was depopulated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, who overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> in 750, were less expansionist, yet clashes continued almost every year until the mid-10th century. Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 827 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> gradually from 831 to 878. In 863 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronas">Petronas</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lalakaon">Battle of Lalakaon</a>, beginning a century of Byzantine offensives under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>, and Crete was reconquered in 961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the end of the 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> had replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> as the major Arab power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and halted further Byzantine advances. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> had lost much territory, and their focus shifted from the western reconquests of Justinian to defence against the Islamic armies in the east. The war strained relations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Byzantium, since the empire could no longer protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> as it once had.</p><p>Walter Emil Kaegi notes that the Arabic sources contain obscurities and contradictions, and that Byzantine chronicles such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes</a> and Nicephorus are terse and biased, so neither tradition can serve as the standard for the other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arab–Byzantine wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
